// Yggdrasil stage-1 shake, as a Datalog rule set.
//
// This is the oracle half of docs/analysis-rules.md stage 1: it recomputes
// the shake's footprint from facts dumped by `yggdrasil facts PROG DIR`
// (yggdrasil.shen, (yggdrasil.facts ...)) and must agree, set for set, with
// the defuns the shake writes to kernel.kl -- excluding the synthesised
// shen.initialise, which is manufactured at write time and has no row in the
// call graph.
//
//     yggdrasil facts tests/fib.shen facts
//     souffle -F facts -D out analysis/analysis.dl
//     sort out/reach.csv
//
// Nothing here runs in a user's shake. Soufflé is a differential oracle in
// CI; analysis/refeval.py evaluates the same rules in stdlib Python for
// local runs. Both must produce the same reach set.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// DEVIATIONS FROM THE NOTE
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// docs/analysis-rules.md was written from the design of the shake; these
// rules are written from `called-fns`, `prepare-tops`, `strip-f-error-row`
// and `trim-top` as they actually stand in yggdrasil.shen. Where the two
// disagree, the code wins and the divergence is recorded here.
//
// D1. The note's edge rules are position-sensitive:
//         edge(F,G) :- callpos(F,G), kernel(G).
//         edge(F,G) :- argpos(F,G,C), kernel(G), C != cons.
//     The code is not. `called-fns` is a plain cons walk that collects EVERY
//     kernel-defined symbol leaf of a body, in any position, cons arguments
//     included (docs/reachability.md calls this out as a deliberate
//     over-approximation). So the `C != cons` side condition is dropped:
//     argpos yields an edge unconditionally, exactly like callpos. The
//     callpos/argpos split is kept in the facts because it is what makes the
//     rules legible, and because stage 3 may want to narrow the edge rule --
//     but narrowing it today would shrink the footprint, which stage 1 is
//     not allowed to do.
//
// D2. The note gates data-literal symbols on eval-capability:
//         edge(F,G) :- datasym(F,G), kernel(G), evalcapable.
//     The code has no such gate. `called-fns`'s four exceptions -- the arity
//     table literal, the `put P shen.external-symbols` registration, a
//     toplevel `(declare F Type)`, and the `shen.*special*` /
//     `shen.*extraspecial*` lists, plus the `shen.assoc-> K` key -- drop
//     those symbols in BOTH modes. (What is eval-gated is what happens to
//     the surviving literals at write time: `trim-top` filters them to the
//     footprint, which cannot change the footprint.) So `datasym` derives no
//     edge at all. It is dumped and declared anyway: it is the exact record
//     of what the shake decided not to follow, which is the thing a reader
//     of the rule set most wants to see, and stage 3 will need it to prove
//     the exceptions are what the rules say they are.
//
// D3. The note has a fourth edge rule for shen.f-error:
//         edge(shen.f-error, G) :- callpos(shen.f-error, G), evalcapable.
//     That is right in spirit but incomplete: `strip-f-error-row` empties
//     shen.f-error's row wholesale in eval-free mode, so the argpos edges
//     have to go too. Both rules below carry the mode guard.
//
// D4. The note's `seed`/`floorseed` read `form-mentions(N, G)` from a single
//     set of toplevel forms. `prepare-tops` makes the toplevel forms
//     themselves mode-dependent: in eval-free mode it deletes every
//     `(declare F Type)` form and rewrites two more (`(set *macros* ...)` is
//     blanked, `(shen.build-lambda-table ...)` becomes a placeholder). The
//     dump therefore carries both readings, `formmentions` (raw) and
//     `formmentionsef` (post-prepare-tops), indexed by the same raw form
//     number, and the rules pick between them on the mode.
//
// D5. Likewise `usersym`. `strip-user-declares` drops user `(declare ...)`
//     forms in eval-free mode only, so the dump carries `usersym` (the
//     symbols of the user KL as the shake sees it in eval-free mode) and
//     `rawsym` (as it sees it in eval-capable mode). `evalcapable` is
//     derived from `rawsym`, because `eval-free?` is tested against the
//     UNstripped user KL.
//
// D6. `reach` is restricted to `kernel`. The Shen `footprint` puts every
//     seed in its visited set, kernel-defined or not; `footcode` then keeps
//     only the kernel defuns. Restricting the recursion is the same answer
//     one step earlier.
//
// D7. `initprim` is handed over as a fact rather than derived. `trim-top`
//     rewrites the arity table, the external-symbols list and the lambda
//     table against the FOOTPRINT, so the primitives the emitted initialiser
//     mentions depend on the answer this rule set computes. Deriving it
//     would mean restating `trim-arity-pairs`, `keep-set` and
//     `lambdatable-entries` in Datalog for no gain: `reach` is the stage-1
//     acceptance criterion, and `needsEval`/`reaches` ride along with it.
//     Note that `initprim` is consequently dumped for the mode the program
//     is actually in, unlike the other relations.

// ============================== facts ==================================

.decl kernel(f:symbol)                       // F is a kernel defun (a call-graph row head)
.input kernel

.decl callpos(f:symbol, g:symbol)            // G is the head cell of a cons inside F's body
.input callpos

.decl argpos(f:symbol, g:symbol, c:symbol)   // G is a later cell of a call whose head is C
.input argpos

.decl datasym(f:symbol, g:symbol)            // G sits in a subtree called-fns discards (D2)
.input datasym

.decl mentionsprim(f:symbol, p:symbol)       // primitive P occurs in kernel defun F's body
.input mentionsprim

.decl top(n:number, name:symbol)             // the N-th raw kernel toplevel init form
.input top

.decl formmentions(n:number, g:symbol)       // called-fns of raw form N
.input formmentions

.decl formmentionsef(n:number, g:symbol)     // called-fns of form N after prepare-tops (D4)
.input formmentionsef

.decl rawsym(s:symbol)                       // every symbol of the unstripped user KL
.input rawsym

.decl usersym(s:symbol)                      // every symbol of the declare-stripped user KL (D5)
.input usersym

.decl entry(s:symbol)                        // S is in *eval-entry-points*
.input entry

.decl prim(p:symbol)                         // P is in *primitives*
.input prim

.decl cap(c:symbol, p:symbol)                // capability C is gated by primitive P
.input cap

.decl portGlobal(v:symbol)                   // V is supplied by the port (*stinput*, *stoutput*)
.input portGlobal

.decl initprim(p:symbol)                     // primitives of the emitted initialiser (D7)
.input initprim

// ================================ mode =================================
// eval-free? tests the user KL's symbols against *eval-entry-points*. One
// hit is enough; evalcapable names every hit, which is what the
// "eval-capable because the program mentions:" line of `yggdrasil why`
// prints.

.decl evalcapable(s:symbol)
.output evalcapable
evalcapable(S) :- rawsym(S), entry(S).

// anyeval/evalfree are the boolean the rest of the rules branch on. They are
// one-column so that the negation below is over a ground atom.
.decl anyeval(x:number)
anyeval(1) :- evalcapable(_).

.decl evalfree(x:number)
evalfree(1) :- !anyeval(1).

// =============================== edges =================================
// One edge per kernel-defined symbol leaf of a body, in any position (D1),
// minus shen.f-error's whole row when the program is eval-free (D3).

.decl edge(f:symbol, g:symbol)
edge(F, G) :- callpos(F, G), kernel(G), F != "shen.f-error".
edge(F, G) :- argpos(F, G, _), kernel(G), F != "shen.f-error".
edge("shen.f-error", G) :- callpos("shen.f-error", G), kernel(G), anyeval(1).
edge("shen.f-error", G) :- argpos("shen.f-error", G, _), kernel(G), anyeval(1).

// =============================== seeds =================================
// The kernel's own toplevel init forms seed the floor; the user KL seeds the
// rest. Both readings of each are in the facts; the mode picks one (D4, D5).

.decl floorseed(g:symbol)
floorseed(G) :- formmentionsef(_, G), kernel(G), evalfree(1).
floorseed(G) :- formmentions(_, G), kernel(G), anyeval(1).

.decl seed(g:symbol)
seed(G) :- floorseed(G).
seed(G) :- usersym(G), kernel(G), evalfree(1).
seed(G) :- rawsym(G), kernel(G), anyeval(1).

// ============================== reach ==================================
// The worklist traversal of yggdrasil.shen's `reach`, as least fixpoint.
// `reach` IS the acceptance criterion: it must equal kernel.kl's defun list
// minus shen.initialise. `floor` is the same traversal from the init forms
// alone -- what every program pays, the floor= figure of `yggdrasil why`.

.decl reach(g:symbol)
.output reach
reach(G) :- seed(G).
reach(G) :- reach(F), edge(F, G).

.decl floor(g:symbol)
.output floor
floor(G) :- floorseed(G).
floor(G) :- floor(F), edge(F, G).

// ====================== primitives and capabilities ====================
// find-primitives runs over the emitted code: the reachable defuns (after
// rewrite-f-error), the synthesised initialiser, and the user KL.

.decl usedprim(p:symbol)
usedprim(P) :- prim(P), reach(F), mentionsprim(F, P), F != "shen.f-error".
usedprim(P) :- prim(P), reach("shen.f-error"), mentionsprim("shen.f-error", P), anyeval(1).
// rewrite-f-error swaps the eval-free shen.f-error body for
// (simple-error (cn (str V) "...")), so those two primitives replace its own.
usedprim("simple-error") :- reach("shen.f-error"), evalfree(1).
usedprim("cn") :- reach("shen.f-error"), evalfree(1).
usedprim(P) :- initprim(P).
usedprim(P) :- prim(P), usersym(P), evalfree(1).
usedprim(P) :- prim(P), rawsym(P), anyeval(1).

// A capability is reachable exactly when the emitted KL mentions one of its
// gateway primitives -- the manifest's reaches= line.
.decl reaches(c:symbol)
.output reaches
reaches(C) :- cap(C, P), usedprim(P).

.decl needsEval(x:number)
.output needsEval
needsEval(1) :- usedprim("eval-kl").

// portGlobal is declared and read but unused by the stage-1 rules: it is the
// input the stage-2 initialisation-order check (readBeforeWrite) needs, and
// dumping it now keeps the fact set stable across that change.
.decl portGlobalUnused(v:symbol)
portGlobalUnused(V) :- portGlobal(V), V = "never".
